# How many composites in GLOBAL virtual memory a program can reach and write,
# and a digest of which ones.
#
# A restore does not reach global virtual memory (PLRM 3.7.2), so a write into
# one of these stands for the rest of the job -- and the machinery's own
# namespaces are reachable, because bind freezes the dictionary a helper names
# into the helper's body and a program can read that body. An object with all
# three properties is one a program can rewrite and the machinery will then run
# or read.
#
# THERE ARE NO PATHS HERE, AND THERE MUST NOT BE. A list of the objects that
# still have the property is a map for whoever would like to use them, and this
# tree is published. The two numbers hold the set without describing it: the
# digest changes if the set changes and the count says which way. Someone
# working on it runs tests/vm_forbidden_test.ps locally, where the sweep will
# name them and nothing is published. The same reason keeps the paths out of
# the test's own output: the guard runs in public build logs.
#
# The count is a RATCHET. It may fall and it may not rise, and either way the
# pair below is rewritten in the commit that moved it. Nothing about the claim
# depends on anyone remembering it.
#
# The digest is order-independent -- each path is folded to a number and the
# numbers are summed -- so the order the walk took does not enter into it, and
# both object widths answer the same pair.
#
# The second number is the storage the language hands programs to write: the
# resource registry and its per-category instance stores, which defineresource
# writes (PLRM 3.9) and which hold procedure sets. They answer the forbidden
# shape while being exactly what a program is entitled to write, so they are
# declared to the sweep instead of being sealed. That declaration is a hole by
# construction, so its SIZE is ratcheted too -- it may fall and it may not
# rise -- and the walk still descends through them, so what a resource holds is
# held to the rule like anything else.
#
# The third pair is the same question asked of LOCAL virtual memory, rooted at
# the machinery's namespaces alone and not at systemdict -- systemdict reaches
# userdict, and so reaches whatever the running program has defined, which
# would make the number move whenever the test was edited. A write into local
# virtual memory is undone by a restore and by the job boundary, so it buys a
# program the rest of the job rather than all of it; for a workload that is one
# job per request, that is the whole of the request. It is held apart from the
# first pair because a single number would hide the stronger of the two claims.
#
#   count  digest

0 0

declared 52

local 0 0

# The fourth pair is the constants bind froze into the machinery's bodies:
# a dictionary, string or literal array that sits inside a procedure, holds
# no executable array, and can be written. The sweep above passes over them
# because nothing there can be made to RUN -- but they are what the
# machinery reads to decide how to read what a program gave it, and a
# program that reads a body reaches one. Sealing them is not automatic: the
# tables the machinery writes at every render live among them, and a rule
# that took the lot stops the interpreter working. So they are counted
# rather than sealed, and the count is a ratchet: it may fall, as each is
# either sealed where it is defined or answered for, and it may not rise.
#
#   count  digest

frozen 0 0

# The other half of that question, and the one place the rule above gives
# way. The job store is where the machinery keeps what it writes as it runs,
# and a body reaches it through a reference baked in at load: state reached
# through code is still state, so the constant rule passes over it. A hole
# nothing counts is a hole nobody can hold to a size, so it is counted here,
# and the pair below is a ratchet like the others -- it may fall and it may
# not rise, and either way the commit that moved it says why.
#
# The two counts partition what a body reaches and is still writable: an
# object leaving one of them has to appear in the other, so state cannot be
# quietly reclassified as a constant, nor a constant hidden in the store.
#
#   count  digest

shielded 214 55832

# What the INTERPRETER can reach that the walk above cannot. Every number
# here describes the graph five named roots reach, and this one says how
# much of the arena that is: the collector marks from the roots the
# interpreter itself has -- the stacks, the save stack, the name stack --
# and what it reaches that the walk did not is a root the walk has not been
# given. Taken at the interpreter's safe point, because marking from inside
# an operator reads a root set that operator's own C frames hold storage
# out of.
#
# Only containers are counted. A dictionary or an array can hold what the
# machinery runs, so one the walk never sees is a gap in the claim; a string
# holds nothing, and the ones outside are the text of interned names, which
# the name tree holds and no object graph reaches. Counting those beside
# these would bury a number that matters under one that moves whenever a
# name is interned.
#
# It is nothing, and the roots are why. The context holds objects in fields
# of its own -- the arc procedure, the page device, the file a run wrapped
# around its program -- which no dictionary names and so no list written in
# PostScript can reach; they are seeded from the X-macro that declares them,
# so a root added to the context joins the walk without this being
# revisited. A ratchet like the others: it may fall and it may not rise, and
# either way the commit that moved it says why.

# What execute-only has closed: a constant a body froze in that no route
# reaches through readable containers, so a program can make the machinery
# run past it and cannot take it. It is NOT the hazard the frozen count is,
# and it is counted anyway -- a census that stopped seeing behind an
# execute-only body would report a hazard closed and a question abandoned
# with the same number, and the two must not read alike. Sealing a body
# execute-only moves objects from `frozen` to here, one for one; the pair
# was demonstrated by doing exactly that, with the sweep's seal held off so
# that there was something left to move.
#
# This one is NOT a downward ratchet: it rises as bodies are closed, which
# is the direction the campaign wants. What must not rise is `frozen`.
#
#   count  digest

closed 0 0

# The machinery's own bodies, and whether a program can still read one.
# A body is what the machinery RUNS; bind freezes the dictionary a helper
# names into the helper's body, so a body a program can read is a
# namespace a program can reach, whatever the namespace's own access
# says. Execute-only (PLRM 3.3.2) leaves the machinery able to run a body
# and takes the reading away.
#
# What is closed is a body stored under a name, in either bank. A body
# nested inside another one is left read-only: it is not a route of its
# own, reaching it means reading the body above it, and bind leaves
# nested procedures read-only by design (PLRM 8.2) with the machinery
# reading that structure as it works.
#
# Asked of the machinery's own namespaces and not at systemdict, for the
# reason the local pair is: systemdict reaches userdict, and a number
# that counted the asking program's own procedures would move whenever
# the test that reads it was edited.
#
# What is left is the default colour rendering dictionary's procedure
# arrays -- the three of TransformPQR and the three of EncodeABC, and the
# procedures inside them. A colour rendering dictionary is a program's to
# read (PLRM 7.1), so these answer to the language rather than to the
# machinery, and they are counted rather than exempted: a number that
# stopped seeing them would read the same as one with nothing to see.
#
# A ratchet: it may fall and it may not rise.
#
#   count  digest

bodies 15 16217

# Tables in LOCAL virtual memory that a program can write and the machinery
# can be made to run out of. The pair above them says `local 0 0`, and that
# is not the claim it looks like: the seal reduces a dictionary only in
# global memory, because the local ones are where the machinery keeps what
# it writes at every render and sealing them stops the interpreter working
# -- MEASURED, 101 tests fail. So the local pair's dictionary rule ends in
# "and global" and can only ever report an executable array.
#
# What this counts is real and was demonstrated: a program replaced Emit in
# a device class dictionary and the machinery ran the program's procedure
# at showpage. A restore and the job boundary both take local memory back,
# so it buys the rest of the job -- which, for one job per request, is the
# request.
#
# The device classes were the bulk of this and are now closed, not by
# splitting them but by removing what wrote them. A class held two things
# it should not have: the page geometry a Create staged through it on the
# way to the instance, and the band budget a run settled on. Both belong
# to the device being made, and go there now, so a class is settled once
# the boot has built it and is sealed at the lockdown. Nothing was split
# and no lookup was added to the dispatch.
#
# What is left is the graphics state. It holds procedures a program sets
# -- a transfer, a spot function -- and is written on every gsave, so it
# is state rather than a table to close, and closing it is a different
# question with a different answer.
#
# A run that has asked to instrument the machinery keeps the classes
# writable and is counted here as any run would be; the number below is
# an ordinary run's, which is the one that ships.
#
# This is a ratchet: it may fall and it may not rise.
#
#   count  digest

localcode 2 41351

blind 0
